cfg - read and change system settings

  cfg                    every domain and key
  cfg net                one domain
  cfg set input.keymap be_azerty
  cfg reset input.keymap

Keys are declared by the system; a name it does not know is an error,
not a new setting. Changes reach whoever watches the domain at once.
The store says plainly when the root is memory and a change will not
outlive the boot.

Settings are read from /etc first and then from /cfg on top, so a value
nobody has changed is the one the system was built with and a value
changed is the one that was chosen. Writing goes to /cfg alone, which is
a volume on the boot medium rather than part of the root filesystem: it
is what makes a choice outlive the machine being switched off. On a
machine whose /cfg is not mounted, a change is refused rather than kept
for a session and quietly lost.

